# Semantic partition by item identifier

Read the actual meaning of each numbered item and partition the entire collection into useful recurring themes, up to maximum_groups.
Return the required JSON object with:
- labels: an array of short, distinct theme labels, each within max_length characters. Group identifiers are their one-based positions.
- assignments: for EVERY source item identifier, the integer identifier of its single best-fitting group.

Use fewer groups when there are fewer genuinely distinct themes. Never exceed maximum_groups or force trivial splits.
The identifiers preserve source text. They are references, not ranks or content.
Do not group by identifier order, prose style, source person, punctuation or a common domain such as "personality."
Distinguish meaningful themes present across the collection. Let the themes emerge from the items rather than imposing demographic stereotypes.
A theme can combine compatible related concepts when the group limit requires it. Use every group for at least one item.

Read each item as a whole; assign it according to its main substantive meaning.
Mixed items go to the most fitting theme, not multiple groups.
Use the same thematic distinction consistently throughout the collection.
The deterministic caller reconstructs the complete, unchanged original strings, including duplicate occurrences.
You therefore must NOT repeat or rewrite any source text in the output.

Check that the labels genuinely distinguish their assigned members.
Do not use a universal category that erases the differences, and do not fill a last category with leftovers unrelated to its label.
Return only schema-conforming JSON.
